# Task: chebyshev_mish_d7
Degree-7 Chebyshev polynomial approximation of the mish activation on [-4, 4] (benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks).
Inputs: ciphertext `x` (values in [-4, 4]); plaintext scalars: `alpha` = 0.25 (maps x to z = x/4 in [-1, 1]), `one` = 1.0, and c0 ... c7, the Chebyshev coefficients (each one is the same in every slot).
Output: `y` = c0 + sum_k c_k T_k(z), with T0 = 1, T1 = z and T_k = 2 z T_{k-1} - T_{k-2}. The coefficients c1, c2, c3, c4, c5, c6, c7 and c0 are non-negligible; the rest are ~1e-16 and can be dropped.
Any mathematically equivalent evaluation is allowed (another basis or evaluation order), as long as it only uses these inputs.

Correctness: every output must be within 1e-06 (absolute) of the expected values on all test cases (fixed, dense). Timing is measured on the dense case.

# Baseline kernel
```cpp
// Baseline generated by agent/tasks/gen_baselines.py; Chebyshev mish degree 7 on [-4, 4], as in benchmarks/chebyshev_ckks.

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Plaintext one("one");
  vector<Ciphertext> t(8);
  t[1] = z;
  Ciphertext z_square = z * z;
  t[2] = z_square + z_square - one;
  for (int k = 3; k <= 7; ++k)
  {
    Ciphertext z_t = z * t[k - 1];
    t[k] = z_t + z_t - t[k - 2];
  }
  Ciphertext result;
  bool has_result = false;
  Plaintext c1("c1");
  Ciphertext term1 = t[1] * c1;
  result = has_result ? result + term1 : term1;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c2("c2");
  Ciphertext term2 = t[2] * c2;
  result = has_result ? result + term2 : term2;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c3("c3");
  Ciphertext term3 = t[3] * c3;
  result = has_result ? result + term3 : term3;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c4("c4");
  Ciphertext term4 = t[4] * c4;
  result = has_result ? result + term4 : term4;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c5("c5");
  Ciphertext term5 = t[5] * c5;
  result = has_result ? result + term5 : term5;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c6("c6");
  Ciphertext term6 = t[6] * c6;
  result = has_result ? result + term6 : term6;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c7("c7");
  Ciphertext term7 = t[7] * c7;
  result = has_result ? result + term7 : term7;
  has_result = true;
  Plaintext c0("c0");
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

# Baseline result
status: ok (correct on every case)
fhe() evaluation time (dense case, median): 182.30 ms
CKKS levels consumed: 8
CHEHAB depth (total, multiplicative): (22, 8); IR terms: 85
operations: ct_ct_mul=6, square=0, ct_pt_mul=8, relin=6, rotate=0, rescale=14, mod_switch=10, match_scale=11, add=19
max abs error vs expected: 1.23e-07

# How a kernel is judged
One timing is too noisy to rank kernels: the same circuit can measure several percent faster or slower from run to run. So a correct kernel becomes the new best only if
1. its circuit differs from the baseline's, the current best's and every earlier attempt's (circuit = CHEHAB's depth and complete operation counts), and
2. it is then confirmed faster than the best when both are timed in alternating batches (at least 3% faster, winning most batches).
Rewrites that leave the circuit unchanged (renaming, reordering, regrouping that CHEHAB normalizes away) cannot win. Aim for fewer ciphertext multiplications (including square), rotations, plaintext multiplications, rescales and scale alignments, or fewer levels.

You have 8 attempts. Propose a faster kernel that computes the same outputs.

Idea: convert the Chebyshev series into a power-basis polynomial in x, so the plaintext coefficients can be combined cheaply. The conversion is not possible at compile time, because the coefficients are plaintext inputs. Instead I use an evaluation scheme with few ct-ct multiplications and a low depth.

With z = alpha*x, the polynomial is p(z) = c0 + c1 z + ... + c7 T7(z). The Chebyshev products are the expensive part.

Plan using the product identities T_{m+n} = 2 T_m T_n - T_{|m-n|}:
- z2 = z*z, T2 = 2z2 - 1 (depth 2 after the alpha multiply).
- T3 = 2 z T2 - z, T4 = 2 T2² - 1.
- T4 needs T2² (depth 3).
- T5 = 2 T2 T3 - z, T6 = 2 T3² - 1, T7 = 2 T3 T4 - z.

That is 6 ct-ct multiplies (z², zT2, T2², T2T3, T3², T3T4), the same count as the baseline but with lower depth. T3 sits at depth 3 and T4 at depth 3, so T7 lands at depth 4. Each ct-pt coefficient multiply adds one more level, giving depth 5 against 8.

Reducing multiplies further: to avoid the separate alpha multiply, I compute z = x*alpha (one plaintext multiply). The coefficient multiplies are 7 ct-pt, with c1 folded into the z term.

The scale subtraction rules need care: "2*z2 - one" subtracts a plaintext, which is fine. For T3 = 2 z T2 - z, z is at a shallower level, and CHEHAB aligns it. The sums of the c_k terms are at different levels, which is fine.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2s + t2s - one;
  Ciphertext a5 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t5 = a5 + a5 - z;
  Ciphertext s3 = square(t3);
  Ciphertext t6 = s3 + s3 - one;
  Ciphertext a7 = t3 * t4;
  Ciphertext t7 = a7 + a7 - z;

  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext result = z * c1;
  result = result + t2 * c2;
  result = result + t3 * c3;
  result = result + t4 * c4;
  result = result + t5 * c5;
  result = result + t6 * c6;
  result = result + t7 * c7;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The previous attempt failed because c3·T3 and c4·T4 reach the same level with different scale histories. The fix is to reorder the sums so terms only meet when their levels differ or their structure matches. I group c4·T4 with the deeper terms (c5, c6, c7) and add the shallow group c1·z + c2·T2 + c3·T3 last. The circuit itself is unchanged.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2s + t2s - one;
  Ciphertext a5 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t5 = a5 + a5 - z;
  Ciphertext s3 = square(t3);
  Ciphertext t6 = s3 + s3 - one;
  Ciphertext a7 = t3 * t4;
  Ciphertext t7 = a7 + a7 - z;

  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext hi = t4 * c4;
  hi = hi + t5 * c5;
  hi = hi + t6 * c6;
  hi = hi + t7 * c7;
  Ciphertext lo = z * c1;
  lo = lo + t2 * c2;
  lo = lo + t3 * c3;
  Ciphertext result = hi + lo;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The earlier failure came from adding c4·T4 to c5·T5 before c6·T6. That mixed their scale histories at level 5. This time I sum c5·T5, c6·T6 and c7·T7 first, hoping they share a history. Then I add c4·T4 and the shallow group c1·z + c2·T2 + c3·T3.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2s + t2s - one;
  Ciphertext a5 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t5 = a5 + a5 - z;
  Ciphertext s3 = square(t3);
  Ciphertext t6 = s3 + s3 - one;
  Ciphertext a7 = t3 * t4;
  Ciphertext t7 = a7 + a7 - z;

  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext hi = t5 * c5;
  hi = hi + t6 * c6;
  hi = hi + t7 * c7;
  Ciphertext mid = t4 * c4;
  Ciphertext lo = z * c1;
  lo = lo + t2 * c2;
  lo = lo + t3 * c3;
  Ciphertext result = hi + mid;
  result = result + lo;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The mismatch is between c5·T5 and c6·T6, because T6 = 2·T3² − 1 has a heavier scale history than T5. I switch to T6 = 2·T2·T4 − T2, so T6 has the same structure as T5 (a product of T2 and a depth-3 term). I keep the same operation counts and sum the terms in a different order.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2s + t2s - one;
  Ciphertext a5 = t2 * t3;
  Ciphertext t5 = a5 + a5 - z;
  Ciphertext a6 = t2 * t4;
  Ciphertext t6 = a6 + a6 - t2;
  Ciphertext a7 = t3 * t4;
  Ciphertext t7 = a7 + a7 - z;

  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext hi = t5 * c5;
  hi = hi + t6 * c6;
  Ciphertext mid = t4 * c4;
  Ciphertext lo = z * c1;
  lo = lo + t2 * c2;
  lo = lo + t3 * c3;
  Ciphertext result = hi + mid;
  result = result + lo;
  result = result + t7 * c7;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Mixing T3 and T4 at the same level is what fails, so I keep distinct levels per term. I use T4 = 2·T2² − 1, which is shallower than the baseline chain. T5, T6 and T7 continue the chain from z, so each lands at its own level. The only same-level pair is c3·T3 and c4·T4, so I add c4·T4 early and c3·T3 only after the running sum has reached a deeper level.

```cpp
void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext z2 = square(z);
  Ciphertext t2 = z2 + z2 - one;
  Ciphertext zt2 = z * t2;
  Ciphertext t3 = zt2 + zt2 - z;
  Ciphertext t2s = square(t2);
  Ciphertext t4 = t2s + t2s - one;
  Ciphertext z4 = z * t4;
  Ciphertext t5 = z4 + z4 - t3;
  Ciphertext z5 = z * t5;
  Ciphertext t6 = z5 + z5 - t4;
  Ciphertext z6 = z * t6;
  Ciphertext t7 = z6 + z6 - t5;

  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");
  Ciphertext result = z * c1;
  result = result + t2 * c2;
  result = result + t4 * c4;
  result = result + t5 * c5;
  result = result + t6 * c6;
  result = result + t7 * c7;
  result = result + t3 * c3;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

The previous kernel kept all six ct-ct products and was slower. This one uses an even/odd split in u = z². With u, u² and u³, each T_k or T_k/z is a small integer-coefficient polynomial in u, built from adds and `one`. That leaves 4 ct-ct multiplies instead of 6, with 6 coefficient multiplies plus the alpha one, and a depth of 6. The result is y = c0 + E(u) + z·(c1 + O(u)).

```cpp
Ciphertext mul_int(const Ciphertext &a, int n)
{
  Ciphertext r;
  bool h = false;
  Ciphertext p = a;
  while (n)
  {
    if (n & 1)
    {
      r = h ? r + p : p;
      h = true;
    }
    n >>= 1;
    if (n)
      p = p + p;
  }
  return r;
}

void fhe()
{
  Ciphertext x("x");
  Plaintext alpha("alpha");
  Plaintext one("one");
  Plaintext c0("c0"), c1("c1"), c2("c2"), c3("c3"), c4("c4"), c5("c5"), c6("c6"), c7("c7");

  Ciphertext z = x * alpha;
  Ciphertext u = square(z);
  Ciphertext u2 = square(u);
  Ciphertext u3 = u * u2;

  // even part
  Ciphertext T2 = mul_int(u, 2) - one;
  Ciphertext T4 = mul_int(u2, 8) - mul_int(u, 8) + one;
  Ciphertext T6 = mul_int(u3, 32) - mul_int(u2, 48) + mul_int(u, 18) - one;
  // odd part (divided by z)
  Ciphertext W3 = mul_int(u, 4) - one - one - one;
  Ciphertext W5 = mul_int(u2, 16) - mul_int(u, 20);
  W5 = W5 + one + one + one + one + one;
  Ciphertext W7 = mul_int(u3, 64) - mul_int(u2, 112) + mul_int(u, 56);
  W7 = W7 - one - one - one - one - one - one - one;

  Ciphertext E = T2 * c2;
  E = E + T4 * c4;
  E = E + T6 * c6;

  Ciphertext O = W3 * c3;
  O = O + W5 * c5;
  O = O + W7 * c7;
  O = O + c1;

  Ciphertext result = E + z * O;
  result = result + c0;
  result.set_output("y");
}
```

Attempt 6 spent about 26 plaintext constant adds (~2.5 ms each) on the integer constants. I change variable to v = T2 = 2z² − 1, which turns the polynomials into:
- T4 = 2v² − 1
- T6 = 4v³ − 3v
- T3/z = 2v − 1
- T5/z = 4v² − 2v − 1
- T7/z = 8v³ + 4v² − 4v − 1

That leaves only 5 `one` constant ops. The ct-ct multiply count stays at 4.

```cpp
